<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maastricht Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1992">1992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Treaty on European Union, Treaty of Maastricht</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> Treaty, formally the Treaty on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a>, was signed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> city of Maastricht in February 1992 and came into force in November 1993. It created the European Union, introduced European citizenship and set out the path to economic and monetary union and the euro. It also added common foreign and security policy and justice and home affairs cooperation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maastricht-treaty.jpg" alt="Maastricht Treaty" width="320" height="161" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mateus2019 · CC BY 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GER_%E2%80%94_BY_%E2%80%94_Regensburg_-_Donaumarkt_1_(Museum_der_Bayerischen_Geschichte;_Vertrag_von_Maastricht)_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The treaty was negotiated against the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> and the reunification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and had to reconcile member states seeking deeper integration with those wishing to keep national control. French President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-mitterrand">François Mitterrand</a> had been forced to abandon his Socialist programme in 1983 because of speculation against the franc, and he was afterwards committed to drawing Germany into a currency partnership. When Chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helmut-kohl">Helmut Kohl</a> asked for reunification in 1990, Mitterrand would accept it only if Germany agreed to give up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deutsche-mark">Deutsche Mark</a> for a common currency.</p><p>The twelve members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Communities</a> signed the treaty on 7 February 1992. It was signed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a> because the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> held the presidency of the Council during the six months of negotiation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Title I established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> on the foundation of the three partly merged communities, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-economic-community">European Economic Community</a>, the European Coal and Steel Community and Euratom. Its objectives were a citizenship of the Union, economic and monetary union ultimately with a single currency, and a common foreign and security policy. Title II renamed the European Economic Community the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-communities">European Community</a> and set a staged path to monetary union, with criteria for adopting the single currency and a future European Central Bank.</p><p>Every national of a member state became a citizen of the Union, with the right to vote and stand in local and European elections where they lived. The European Parliament gained co-decision on some legislation and the power to confirm the Council's nominations for the Commission. Foreign policy and justice and home affairs stayed intergovernmental, and the United Kingdom obtained opt-outs from the social protocol and the final stage of monetary union.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Ratification required referendums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Ireland. Danish voters rejected the treaty on 2 June 1992 by 50.7 to 49.3 percent, but after concessions agreed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, including an exemption from the single currency like the British one, a second referendum on 18 May 1993 approved it with 56.7 percent. Ireland approved it on 18 June 1992 with 69.1 percent, and France narrowly in September 1992 with 50.8 percent. In Britain Prime Minister John Major overcame Conservative rebels only by tying ratification to a vote of confidence. The German Federal Constitutional Court ruled it compatible with the Basic Law on 12 October 1993.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maastricht Treaty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A founding member of the European Economic Community, it signed the <b>Maastricht Treaty</b> in 1992 and the Lisbon Treaty in 2007 and co-founded the eurozone; the European Central Bank sits in Frankfurt, its largest financial centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-germany">Federal Republic of Germany · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1945 the Netherlands became a founding member of Benelux, NATO, the European Coal and Steel Community and the European Economic Community, and the <b>Treaty of Maastricht</b> of 1992 that created the European Union was signed on Dutch soil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Kingdom of the Netherlands · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty on European Union</b> came into force, establishing the EU alongside the European Communities.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-1">1 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
